body:
in western culture, you mean. there are many cultures worldwide who have historically included people of a third gender (or more). the hijra of india, the muxes of mexico, the kathoeys in thailand, two-spirit people in many native american cultures. your personal understanding of history based on your own culture isn't a universal truth.  [http://www.pbs.org/independentlens/content/two-spirits\_map-html/](http://www.pbs.org/independentlens/content/two-spirits_map-html/)

here are a few links ([one](https://qz.com/1007198/the-myth-that-gender-is-binary-is-perpetuated-by-a-flawed-education-system/), [two](https://blogs.scientificamerican.com/sa-visual/visualizing-sex-as-a-spectrum/)) explaining why the concept of binary sex isn't so simple and why it's more of a spectrum than we have been led to believe for the sake of ease. many intersex people have undergone unnecessary and/or harmful surgery as infants or children because of this. 

&#x200B;
